Template: passwd/root-password
Type: password
Description: Axonwall Administrator Password:
 Please enter the administrator password for Axonwall. This password will be
 used to log into the Web Console (https://<ip>:2966) and Appliance Console as
 'admin', as well as the underlying system root account.
 .
 Note that you will not be able to see the password as you type it.

Template: passwd/root-password-again
Type: password
Description: Re-enter Axonwall Administrator Password:
 Please enter the administrator password again to verify that you have typed it
 correctly.

Template: axonwall/ab_explanation
Type: note
Description: Axonwall A/B Dual-Root & ZFS Storage Partition Architecture:
 To ensure zero-downtime, unbrickable operating system and firmware upgrades,
 Axonwall implements an enterprise A/B Dual-Root Partition Scheme with an
 autonomous watchdog, optional RAID-1 System Disk Mirroring, and integrated
 OpenZFS Storage Pool provisioning.
 .
 The system drive(s) are partitioned into:
  - Slot A: Primary Active System [axon_root_a]
  - Slot B: Alternate Standby System [axon_root_b]
  - Persistent Data Volume: /var/lib/axonwall [axon_data]
  - Optional ZFS Storage Partition: [axon_zfs] (for NAS & K3s OpenEBS)
 .
 WHY TWO IDENTICAL SYSTEM SLOTS?
 When software or firmware updates are applied, Axonwall installs them onto
 the inactive standby slot in the background while your network remains live.
 Upon reboot, the system verifies hardware health, kernel netfilter readiness,
 and control plane quorum. If a kernel panic occurs or connectivity is lost,
 the watchdog autonomously reverts to the alternate slot within 3 boot attempts.
 .
 Because both system slots are identical, the partition size you select
 in the next step will be allocated TWICE (once for Slot A and once for Slot B).
 You can also mirror the system across multiple drives (RAID-1) and allocate
 remaining disk space (plus any secondary drives) to an OpenZFS pool.

Template: axonwall/system_raid_mode
Type: select
Choices: Single Disk (Standard A/B Dual-Root), RAID-1 Mirror across Multiple Disks (Redundant A/B Dual-Root)
Default: Single Disk (Standard A/B Dual-Root)
Description: Select system disk redundancy mode:
 If your appliance has 2 or more drives, Axonwall can mirror the system
 partitions (Slot A, Slot B, Swap, /var/lib/axonwall, and UEFI/BIOS bootloaders)
 across multiple disks using Linux RAID-1 (mdadm) so the appliance survives
 a complete primary drive failure.

Template: axonwall/system_raid_disks
Type: multiselect
Choices: ${CHOICES}
Description: Select 2 or more disks for the RAID-1 mirrored system volume:
 Choose the physical drives to mirror for the operating system. All selected
 drives will receive identical partition layouts and bootloaders.

Template: axonwall/root_size
Type: select
Choices: 16 GB per slot (Recommended - Standard 32 GB+ drive), 8 GB per slot (Compact - 16-32 GB embedded/eMMC), 32 GB per slot (Expanded - 128 GB+ enterprise SSD), Custom (Enter specific size in GB)
Default: 16 GB per slot (Recommended - Standard 32 GB+ drive)
Description: Select system root partition size (per slot):
 Please choose the partition size for each system slot. Remember that Axonwall
 will allocate this size twice (for Slot A and Slot B):
 .
  - 16 GB per slot: Recommended for most firewall deployments with IDS/IPS.
  - 8 GB per slot: Compact layout for smaller SSDs, CF cards, or eMMC drives.
  - 32 GB per slot: High-capacity layout for heavy container and plugin use.
  - Custom: Enter a specific size in gigabytes.

Template: axonwall/custom_root_size
Type: string
Default: 16
Description: Custom system root partition size (per slot, in GB):
 Enter the desired size in gigabytes for each system slot. Minimum recommended
 size is 6 GB.

Template: axonwall/swap_size
Type: select
Choices: 2 GB Swap (Standard), No swap partition (ZRAM / High-End NVMe), 4 GB Swap (Extended), 8 GB Swap (Large Memory Dump)
Default: 2 GB Swap (Standard)
Description: Select swap space allocation:
 Choose swap partition size for this appliance:
 .
  - 2 GB Swap: Standard allocation for general routing and caching.
  - No swap partition: Recommended when using high-speed NVMe or ZRAM.
  - 4 GB / 8 GB Swap: Extended swap for heavy Suricata DPI or memory buffering.

Template: axonwall/zfs_allocation
Type: select
Choices: Allocate remaining space to ZFS storage (16 GB axon_data + Remainder ZFS), Split remaining space (50% axon_data / 50% ZFS storage), Custom ZFS partition size (Enter specific size in GB), No ZFS partition on system disk (100% remaining space to axon_data)
Default: Allocate remaining space to ZFS storage (16 GB axon_data + Remainder ZFS)
Description: Select ZFS storage allocation on system disk(s):
 Axonwall can carve out a dedicated OpenZFS storage partition (axon_zfs) on
 the system drive(s) for NAS shares (SMB/NFS/iSCSI/S3) and K3s OpenEBS volumes:
 .
  - Allocate remaining space to ZFS: Reserves 16 GB (or 8 GB on compact drives)
    for /var/lib/axonwall and assigns all remaining capacity to OpenZFS.
  - Split remaining space (50/50): Divides remaining capacity equally between
    /var/lib/axonwall (ext4) and OpenZFS storage.
  - Custom ZFS partition size: Specify the exact size in GB for ZFS on the
    system disk(s).
  - No ZFS partition on system disk: Assigns 100% of remaining space on the
    system disk to /var/lib/axonwall (use this for pure firewalls or when using
    separate secondary disks exclusively for ZFS).

Template: axonwall/custom_zfs_size
Type: string
Default: 32
Description: Custom ZFS partition size on system disk(s) (in GB):
 Enter the desired size in gigabytes for the dedicated ZFS storage partition
 (axon_zfs) on each system disk.

Template: axonwall/zfs_extra_disks
Type: multiselect
Choices: ${CHOICES}
Description: Select additional dedicated disks for the ZFS storage pool (Optional):
 If you have additional dedicated storage drives to include in the 'tank'
 OpenZFS pool, select them below. Leave unselected to use only the system
 disk ZFS partition(s).

Template: axonwall/zfs_raid_topology
Type: select
Choices: Auto (Recommended based on disk/partition count), Stripe (RAID-0 - Max capacity, no parity), Mirror (RAID-1 / RAID-10 - 2+ VDEVs, 1-drive fault tolerance), RAID-Z1 (Single Parity - min 3 VDEVs, 1-drive fault tolerance), RAID-Z2 (Dual Parity - min 4 VDEVs, 2-drive fault tolerance), RAID-Z3 (Triple Parity - min 5 VDEVs, 3-drive fault tolerance), dRAID1 (Distributed RAID-Z1 - min 4 VDEVs, integrated spare), dRAID2 (Distributed RAID-Z2 - min 6 VDEVs, dual parity + integrated spare)
Default: Auto (Recommended based on disk/partition count)
Description: Select OpenZFS pool ('tank') RAID topology:
 Choose the ZFS redundancy level across your selected ZFS partitions and/or
 dedicated storage disks:
 .
  - Auto: Automatically selects Stripe (1 VDEV), Mirror (2 VDEVs),
    RAID-Z1 (3-4 VDEVs), or RAID-Z2 (5+ VDEVs).
  - Mirror: Ideal for 2-disk systems or low-latency IOPS workloads.
  - RAID-Z1 / RAID-Z2 / RAID-Z3: Single, dual, or triple parity across 3+ VDEVs.
  - dRAID1 / dRAID2: Next-gen Distributed RAID-Z with rapid rebuild times.
